{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1788363061,"nonce":"h07zYyk8Fdugh7O86Yl642jHj80=","title":"Evan, 2026-09-02: *\"I'd like to be able to observe while it's going or watch a previous run if we have the data, my cockpit shouldn't actually control anything though, just watch.\"*","message":"Today the only way to see what an agent is doing is to read raw JSON envelopes out of `transcripts/`, or to watch the bench window and see a colony move without knowing why. A 66-day run produced **4,599 transcript steps** and the only tool that reads them is `accept/4.2-play-loop.py`, which grades a finished run rather than showing one.\n\n\nEvery `rwa` call already lands on disk as `transcripts/\u003crun\u003e/NNN-\u003cop\u003e/{cmd.json,result.json}`, and the journal is ndjson. So:\n\n**Live mode is replay mode tailing a directory that is still growing.** Same renderer, same parser, one code path. Replay scrubs a finished chain; live follows the tail.\n\n\n**The cockpit sends nothing. It issues no verb, ever — not even an observer.**\n\n`JournalVerbs.cs:152` states it: *\"Calling this moves `Journal.ReadWatermark`, and the watermark is what `advance` compares against before it will run again.\"* Line 229 names the failure directly — *\"can see another client move it — the shared-watermark hazard\"* — and publishes `watermark_was`/`watermark_moved` so a client can detect another client doing it.\n\nA cockpit that called `journal` would clear the driving agent's watermark and let its next `advance` run past unread events. That is `722c951`'s refusal defeated, and it is the duplicate-driver deadlock that froze run `m1-20260901` twice. A cockpit that called `digest` would also write `commands/` and `results/` files and claim a transcript step in the driver's own run directory.\n\nReading files the mod already wrote has none of these properties. **That is the whole architecture.**\n\n\n1. One tool, two modes. `cockpit \u003crun\u003e` replays a finished run from `transcripts/`; `cockpit --follow \u003crun\u003e` tails a growing one. The renderer and the parser are shared, not duplicated.\n2. **It opens no socket and writes nothing into the bench root.** Proven, not asserted: run it against a live bench for 60s and assert `commands/` gained no file, `results/` gained no file, no new transcript step directory appeared, and the journal's `ReadWatermark` is unchanged. This assertion is the issue's real deliverable.\n3. It walks a segment chain. `5eba561` shipped `meta.json` with `prev`/`next`; run `m1-20260901` is five segments and 4,599 steps. Reading only the head shows 21.7% of the run.\n4. It renders, per step: the op and its args, the envelope (folded, not a wall — `ok`/`error` legible at a glance), the journal delta at that tick, and the `map-view` ASCII when the step has one. Scrub by step and jump by day boundary.\n5. It reads the journal **as a file**, never through the verb.\n6. Degrades honestly: a step whose `result.json` is absent (the call never returned — this is how a wedged run looks) renders as such rather than as an empty envelope. Run `m1-20260901` contains real instances.\n\n\nControl of any kind. Sending, pausing, advancing, answering a dialog. If a control surface is ever wanted it is a separate issue with the watermark question settled first, and `65e7cf9` (no client liveness at all) is its prerequisite.\n\n\nSection C of `RUNS/m1-20260901/next-run.md` opens with *\"I never looked at the base after day 1\"* — 66 days driven entirely off numbers. This is the cheap half of that lesson: it needs no mod change, no bench, and no risk to a running colony, and it works today against 4,599 steps already on disk.","files":null}]}